# Cloudflare Pages response headers. Vite copies public/ verbatim into dist/, so this ships with
# the build.
#
# ONLY the hashed-asset rule is here, deliberately. index.html is already served as
# "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate" by Pages' own defaults -- verified against production -- and
# that is exactly right: the HTML must be revalidated every load, because it is the file that names
# which chunk hashes are current. Restating it here would add a second place to get it wrong.
#
# Worth being clear about what this does and does not fix. It does NOT prevent the stale-chunk
# failure that motivated it. That failure happens to a tab which was ALREADY OPEN across a deploy:
# the module graph in memory holds the old hashes, and client-side navigation never re-fetches the
# HTML, so no cache header on any file can help. src/lib/staleChunk.ts is what handles that. This
# is a straightforward performance change that happens to live next door to it.

/*
  # Security headers for every response (production-readiness audit finding: this file only had
  # a caching rule, so the SPA shipped with none of Cloudflare Pages' security-header handling
  # doing anything meaningful -- Pages does not set a CSP or HSTS on your behalf).
  #
  # Mirrors the policy SecurityConfig.java sets for the API (default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors
  # 'none'; base-uri 'self'), widened only where this app genuinely needs it as a browser-rendered
  # SPA rather than a copy-paste of the API's minimal policy:
  #   - script-src adds https://static.cloudflareinsights.com -- Cloudflare Pages' automatic Web
  #     Analytics injects this beacon into every response at the edge; nothing in this repo
  #     references it. connect-src does NOT need a matching entry: automatic injection reports to
  #     this site's own /cdn-cgi/rum, which 'self' already covers (confirmed against Cloudflare's
  #     Web Analytics FAQ). No inline-script hash/nonce needed either -- the injected snippet
  #     carries its own `integrity` attribute rather than relying on 'unsafe-inline'.
  #   - script-src adds https://www.gstatic.com and https://www.google.com -- Firebase Phone Auth
  #     (src/lib/phoneAuth.ts) renders an invisible reCAPTCHA Enterprise widget. Both domains are
  #     needed: gstatic.com serves supporting assets, but the loader script itself --
  #     recaptcha/enterprise.js -- is served from google.com. Missing google.com here silently
  #     broke every OTP-sending flow (registration, delete-account, phone-change, password-reset
  #     phone-fallback -- everything through phoneAuth.ts) with a generic Firebase
  #     auth/internal-error, since the verifier never finishes initializing without its own script.
  #   - script-src also adds https://accounts.google.com/gsi/client -- D-23 Google Sign-In
  #     (src/lib/googleIdentity.ts) loads Google Identity Services from this exact URL, a DIFFERENT
  #     origin from the reCAPTCHA script above. Bug fix: this was missed when D-23 shipped -- the
  #     button rendered (GoogleSignInButton.tsx has no way to know its own script load was blocked)
  #     but silently failed with "Sign in with Google is unavailable right now" on every real
  #     deployment, since the browser blocked the script outright rather than erroring loudly.
  #     Scoped to the exact path Google's own CSP guide recommends
  #     (developers.google.com/identity/gsi/web/guides/get-google-api-clientid), not the bare
  #     origin, to avoid trusting all of accounts.google.com's script surface for what only needs
  #     this one file.
  #   - style-src adds https://fonts.googleapis.com (index.html's Google Fonts <link>) and
  #     'unsafe-inline' -- this app sets style={{...}} inline styles throughout (28 files). Also
  #     https://accounts.google.com/gsi/style -- GIS's own rendered button loads a stylesheet from
  #     this exact URL, undocumented in Google's own CSP guide above (which covers script-src/
  #     frame-src/connect-src but not this) -- found only by actually serving a real build through
  #     wrangler pages dev and watching the console, not by trusting the docs alone.
  #   - font-src adds https://fonts.gstatic.com, where fonts.googleapis.com's stylesheet actually
  #     points its @font-face src at.
  #   - img-src adds https://img.logo.dev -- BankLogo.tsx/MerchantLogo.tsx's Logo.dev API. Was
  #     https://cdn.brandfetch.io until the Brandfetch->Logo.dev migration (BankLogo.tsx's own
  #     comment history) swapped the provider in code but never updated this allowlist, so every
  #     deployed environment (production and PR previews; the Vite dev server sends no CSP at all)
  #     silently CSP-blocked every Logo.dev image request -- no network entry, no 403, just a
  #     block before the request was even sent, which BankLogo.tsx's circuit breaker then read as
  #     a rejection and used to disable Logo.dev for the rest of that page load.
  #   - connect-src adds api.fynora.net (the deployed backend origin VITE_API_BASE_URL points at --
  #     see src/api/client.ts). api.finoratech.info used to be listed alongside it during the
  #     Fynora domain migration; finoratech.info was sold and is no longer under this project's
  #     control, so it is removed rather than kept as a "just in case" allowance -- there is no
  #     redirect to fall back to, and a CSP entry for a domain someone else now owns is a real
  #     exfiltration path, not a compatibility nicety. Also https://dev-api.fynora.net -- the
  #     dev-tier Railway backend Cloudflare Pages' branch-preview deployments point
  #     VITE_API_BASE_URL at, so sign-in/identify/refresh/register work when manually QA'ing a PR
  #     preview instead of hitting a CSP block. Also identitytoolkit.googleapis.com and
  #     securetoken.googleapis.com (Firebase Auth's own REST endpoints, used by the Firebase Web
  #     SDK the phone-verification flow calls), and Sentry's ingest hosts (src/lib/monitoring.ts,
  #     no-ops unless VITE_SENTRY_DSN is set, but the connect-src entry costs nothing when unused).
  #     Also https://accounts.google.com/gsi/ -- same Google CSP guide above: GIS's own background
  #     requests (session/credential state) go through this path prefix, not a specific named
  #     endpoint, since Google explicitly recommends the prefix over individual GIS URLs so a future
  #     GIS-side change doesn't silently start failing here again.
  #     Bug fix (review): also https://www.google.com -- a SEPARATE gap from the GIS entry above,
  #     missed when #168 fixed GIS's own CSP. reCAPTCHA Enterprise's actual verification call
  #     (recaptcha/enterprise/clr, the network request the widget makes once rendered, not just
  #     the script load already covered by script-src) is fetch/XHR from this page's own context,
  #     not from inside the reCAPTCHA iframe -- so it's connect-src's problem, not frame-src's.
  #     Missing this silently broke every phone-verification send (registration, delete-account,
  #     phone-change, Google Sign-In's own phone step) with the widget rendering fine and the
  #     script loading fine, but the actual verification call blocked -- reproduced live via a
  #     real browser console showing "Connecting to 'https://www.google.com/recaptcha/enterprise/
  #     clr?...' violates... connect-src... The action has been blocked."
  #   - frame-src allows https://www.google.com (the reCAPTCHA challenge iframe Firebase Phone
  #     Auth renders when it can't verify invisibly) and https://accounts.google.com/gsi/ (the
  #     actual "Sign in with Google" button Google renders is itself an iframe from this path, not
  #     just a script -- missing this blocks the button from ever painting even once its script
  #     loads).
  #   - frame-ancestors 'none' takes over X-Frame-Options' job (both do the same thing; setting
  #     both would just be two places to keep in sync).
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://www.gstatic.com https://www.google.com https://accounts.google.com/gsi/client https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com https://accounts.google.com/gsi/style; font-src 'self' data: https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: https://img.logo.dev; connect-src 'self' https://api.fynora.net https://dev-api.fynora.net https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com https://securetoken.googleapis.com https://accounts.google.com/gsi/ https://www.google.com https://*.ingest.sentry.io https://*.ingest.us.sentry.io https://*.ingest.de.sentry.io; frame-src https://www.google.com https://accounts.google.com/gsi/; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

/assets/*
  # Safe to cache forever because the filename IS the content hash: Vite emits a new name whenever
  # the bytes change, so a cached copy can never be wrong -- it can only be unreferenced. Pages
  # defaults these to max-age=14400 with must-revalidate, which makes a returning visitor
  # revalidate every asset after four hours to be told, every time, that nothing changed.
  #
  # `immutable` is the half that matters most: without it a browser still revalidates on reload,
  # which is precisely when a user is most likely to be in a hurry.
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
